## Solution

**Goal.** Evaluate $L = \lim_{n\to\infty}\left(\prod_{k=0}^{n}\binom{n}{k}\right)^{\!\frac{1}{n(n+1)}}$.

**Step 1. Reduce to a logarithmic sum.**

Set $P_n = \prod_{k=0}^{n}\binom{n}{k}$. Then $\log L = \lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{1}{n(n+1)}\sum_{k=0}^{n}\log\binom{n}{k}$, provided this limit exists.

Using $\binom{n}{k}=\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}$,

$$\sum_{k=0}^{n}\log\binom{n}{k}=\sum_{k=0}^{n}\bigl(\log n!-\log k!-\log(n-k)!\bigr)=(n+1)\log n!-2\sum_{k=0}^{n}\log k!,$$

since $\sum_{k=0}^{n}\log k!=\sum_{k=0}^{n}\log(n-k)!$ by the substitution $k\mapsto n-k$.

**Step 2. Rewrite the factorial sum.**

Let $S_n=\sum_{k=1}^{n}\log k!=\sum_{k=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{k}\log j$. Swapping the order of summation,

$$S_n=\sum_{j=1}^{n}(n+1-j)\log j=(n+1)\sum_{j=1}^{n}\log j-\sum_{j=1}^{n}j\log j.$$

Therefore

$$(n+1)\log n!-2S_n=(n+1)\sum_{j=1}^{n}\log j-2\Bigl[(n+1)\sum_{j=1}^{n}\log j-\sum_{j=1}^{n}j\log j\Bigr]=-(n+1)\sum_{j=1}^{n}\log j+2\sum_{j=1}^{n}j\log j.$$

Hence

$$\frac{1}{n(n+1)}\sum_{k=0}^{n}\log\binom{n}{k}=-\underbrace{\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}\log j}_{A_n}+\underbrace{\frac{2}{n(n+1)}\sum_{j=1}^{n}j\log j}_{B_n}.$$

**Step 3. Evaluate $A_n$ via a Riemann sum.**

Write $\log j=\log n+\log\frac{j}{n}$:

$$A_n=\log n+\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}\log\frac{j}{n}\;\longrightarrow\;\log n+\int_{0}^{1}\log x\,dx=\log n-1.$$

**Step 4. Evaluate $B_n$ via a weighted Riemann sum.**

Again $\log j=\log n+\log\frac{j}{n}$, so

$$\sum_{j=1}^{n}j\log j=\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\log n+\sum_{j=1}^{n}j\log\frac{j}{n}.$$

Thus

$$B_n=\log n+\frac{2}{n(n+1)}\sum_{j=1}^{n}j\log\frac{j}{n}=\log n+\frac{2n}{n(n+1)}\sum_{j=1}^{n}\frac{j}{n}\log\frac{j}{n}=\log n+\frac{2}{n+1}\sum_{j=1}^{n}\frac{j}{n}\log\frac{j}{n}.$$

The sum $\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}\frac{j}{n}\log\frac{j}{n}\to\int_{0}^{1}x\log x\,dx=-\frac{1}{4}$, so $\sum_{j=1}^{n}\frac{j}{n}\log\frac{j}{n}\sim -\frac{n}{4}$, and

$$\frac{2}{n+1}\sum_{j=1}^{n}\frac{j}{n}\log\frac{j}{n}\;\longrightarrow\;2\cdot\Bigl(-\frac{1}{4}\Bigr)=-\frac{1}{2}.$$

Therefore $B_n\to\log n-\frac{1}{2}$.

**Step 5. Combine.**

$$-A_n+B_n\;\longrightarrow\;-(\log n-1)+\Bigl(\log n-\frac{1}{2}\Bigr)=\frac{1}{2}.$$

So $\log L=\frac{1}{2}$, and

$$\boxed{L=\sqrt{e}.}$$

